## 8. Orphan / gap audit

**No orphans.** Every element produced is consumed:

| Produced | Consumed by |
|---|---|
| 123 scored field rows | per-goal and per-family aggregates |
| per-goal loss (3) | headline `loss_ratio` |
| per-family loss (8) | Findings F3, F4, F5 (the three worst families) |
| Findings F1–F7 | Conditions C1–C7 — **1:1 onto**, no finding unrouted, no condition ungrounded |
| Conditions C1–C7 | `F-BUNDLE` (C1, C2, C3, C7), `F-ENVELOPE` (C4, C5, C6) — both real backlog nodes with `S-PROBE`/`G-S1-GO` upstream |
| `verdict` + `loss_ratio` | `G-S1-GO`, whose own accept requires a decision citing the loss ratio |
| NO-GO re-test | named follow-up with named candidate goals (B1.C, B2.A, B2.O) and a pre-declared bar |

**No gaps.** Every acceptance command is addressed (§1); every §6.1b bundle node type is
scored; every §4.2 envelope field is scored or explicitly excluded with a spec citation.

**One acknowledged, non-closable gap:** the first accept command tests a path this stage
cannot write. It is not silently absorbed — it is stated in §0, in §1's status table, and in
the completion report, with the exact promotion command. This is a capability boundary, not
a design defect, and closing it inside this stage would require the violation the boundary
exists to prevent.

**Deliberate scope exclusions** (named so they are not mistaken for gaps): ingest, store,
and projection behaviour (downstream of S1); `Problem`/`Objective`/`Mechanism`/`Pattern`
node types (outside the §6.1b bundle); slices S3–S6 (not authorised by the backlog).

